GitHub/GitLab Repository
Step 1: Clone the Repository
What is Clone?

Clone means creating a complete copy of the remote Git repository on your local computer.

The cloned repository contains:

Source code
Branches
Commit history
Configuration files
Command
git clone https://github.com/username/project.git
Example

Suppose the project is stored in GitHub.

GitHub Repository

Inventory-App

Developer clones it:

git clone https://github.com/company/inventory-app.git

Now the entire project is available locally.

Why Clone?
Start development
Get the latest project
Work independently
Make changes locally
Step 2: Source Code

After cloning, the developer starts writing or modifying code.

Example Java project:

Inventory-App

src
 ├── main
 │    ├── java
 │    └── resources
 │
 └── test
      └── java

Common source code files:

Main.java
User.java
Product.java
OrderService.java
application.properties
pom.xml
Developer Activities
Add new features
Fix bugs
Improve performance
Update configuration
Step 3: Compilation
What is Compilation?

Compilation is the process of converting human-readable source code into machine-readable bytecode or object files.

Java source:

Main.java

After compilation:

Main.class

The Java compiler (javac) performs:

Syntax checking
Error detection
Bytecode generation
Maven Command
mvn compile
Gradle Command
gradle build
Output
target/classes/

Example:

target/classes

Main.class
User.class
Order.class
Step 4: Object Files

Object files are the compiled versions of source code.

For Java:

.class files

Example:

User.class

Product.class

Order.class
Purpose
Ready for packaging
Faster execution
Machine understandable
Step 5: Packaging
What is Packaging?

Packaging combines all compiled files and dependencies into a deployable file.

Common package types:

JAR
WAR
EAR
Maven Command
mvn package

Output:

target/

inventory.jar

or

inventory.war
Why Packaging?

Instead of sending hundreds of class files,

User.class

Product.class

Order.class

everything is bundled into one file:

inventory.jar

This is easier to deploy and distribute.

Step 6: Artifact
What is an Artifact?

An Artifact is the final output of the build process.

Examples:

JAR
WAR
ZIP
Docker Image

Example:

inventory.jar

Artifacts are stored in repositories like:

Nexus Repository
JFrog Artifactory
GitHub Packages

Later they are deployed to:

Development
Testing
Staging
Production
Step 7: Unit Testing
What is Unit Testing?

Unit testing verifies that individual methods or classes work correctly.

Tests are written before or after development.

Example:

Calculator

add()

subtract()

multiply()

Unit tests verify:

add(5,3)=8

subtract(5,3)=2

multiply(5,3)=15
Maven Command
mvn test
Benefits
Detect bugs early
Improve code quality
Reduce production failures
Enable safe code changes

Common Java testing frameworks:

JUnit
TestNG
Mockito
Step 8: Git Commit

After successful testing, save the changes locally.

git add .

Stages all modified files.

Commit the changes:

git commit -m "Added login feature"

A commit creates a snapshot of the current code with a descriptive message.

Step 9: Push to GitHub/GitLab

Finally, upload the committed changes to the remote repository.

git push origin main

Now the updated code is available to other developers and can trigger CI/CD pipelines.

Common Git Commands
Command	Purpose
git clone <url>	Download repository
git status	Check file status
git add .	Stage changes
git commit -m "message"	Save changes locally
git push origin main	Upload changes
git pull origin main	Download latest changes
git branch	List branches
git checkout <branch>	Switch branch
Common Maven Commands
Command	Purpose
mvn clean	Remove previous build files
mvn compile	Compile source code
mvn test	Run unit tests
mvn package	Create JAR/WAR
mvn install	Install artifact into the local Maven repository (~/.m2/repository)
mvn deploy	Upload artifact to a remote repository (e.g., Nexus or Artifactory)
Real-Time Example

Suppose you are developing an Employee Management System.

Clone the repository:

git clone https://github.com/company/employee-management.git
Add a new feature, such as employee search.

Compile the code:

mvn compile

Package the application:

mvn package

This generates:

employee-management.jar

Run tests:

mvn test

Commit your changes:

git add .
git commit -m "Added employee search feature"

Push to GitHub:

git push origin feature/employee-search
